          />

          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Software installation graphics, digital assets, and program icons developed from the EKV
            visual system
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Loggy - Character and animation</h2>

      <p>
        Loggy was a more playful project created for a Logina presentation. I developed the mascot
        from the initial idea and name through character sketches, movement studies, storytelling,
        animation, and presentation graphics.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Creating the character</h2>

          <p>
            I conceived and named Loggy as a friendly dog that could make the company’s technology
            feel more approachable. The character needed to work both as a recognisable mascot and
            as an active participant in a short animated story.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Developed the mascot concept and name
            </li>
            <li>
              Sketched the character from several viewpoints
            </li>
            <li>
              Created the finished colour character
            </li>
            <li>
              Designed a playful Loggy wordmark
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-logina-loggy-character-600.webp'
            data-src='design-logina-loggy-character-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='540'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Finished Loggy dog mascot and Loggy wordmark created for Logina'
          />

          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            The finished Loggy mascot and character wordmark
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Preparing Loggy for animation</h2>

          <p>
            I explored the character through hand-drawn studies before creating the movement frames
            required for the presentation. The drawings established Loggy’s proportions,
            personality, expressions, and walking movement.
          </p>

          <p>
            The selected drawings were prepared in Photoshop and developed into a Flash animation. I
            then edited the presentation material in Adobe Premiere and assembled it using Logina’s
            presentation software.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Character sketches and pose development
            </li>
            <li>
              Frame-by-frame movement studies
            </li>
            <li>
              Image preparation in Photoshop
            </li>
            <li>
              Animation in Flash and editing in Premiere
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-logina-loggy-development-600.webp'
            data-src='design-logina-loggy-development-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='620'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Hand-drawn character development and animation studies for the Loggy mascot'
          />

          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Early character studies and movement frames used to develop Loggy for animation
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>A hand-drawn story for the presentation</h2>

      <p>
        I wrote and planned a short story comparing two families passing a Logina information kiosk.
        One family ignores the kiosk and has a difficult day, while the other uses the available
        information and enjoys a better-planned experience. Loggy observes the two different
        journeys.
      </p>

      <p>
        The complete storyboard was drawn by hand with pen on paper before being developed into the
        animated presentation. I also created the artwork for the physical presentation CD and its
        cover.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Visual storytelling</h2>

          <p>
            The presentation used two parallel stories to explain the value of Logina’s public
            information kiosks. One family ignores the kiosk and experiences a difficult day, while
            the other uses its information to plan activities and enjoy a more successful journey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Loggy appears throughout as a friendly observer connecting the scenes and giving the
            presentation humour and personality.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drawing the storyboard first allowed me to plan the sequence, contrast, timing,
            character positions, and transitions before animation production began.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Developed the story and presentation concept
            </li>
            <li>
              Structured two contrasting journeys around the same kiosk
            </li>
            <li>
              Used Loggy to connect the scenes
            </li>
            <li>
              Drew the complete storyboard by hand with pen on paper
            </li>
            <li>
              Designed the accompanying presentation CD artwork
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-logina-loggy-storyboard-600.webp'
            data-src='design-logina-loggy-storyboard-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='809'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Hand-drawn pen-on-paper storyboard for the Loggy animated presentation'
          />

          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            The original storyboard, drawn by hand with pen on paper, comparing two journeys around
            a public information kiosk
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>

      <p>
        This selection represents only part of the work I produced at Logina, but it shows the
        breadth of the role: interaction design, product communication, technical illustration,
        print production, packaging, character development, and animation.
      </p>

      <p>
        Although the visual style belongs to an earlier period of digital design, the underlying
        work remains relevant. Each project required me to understand an unfamiliar technical
        product, work with technical colleagues, organise complex information, and translate it into
        communication suited to its audience and format.
      </p>

      <p class='case-caption'>
        All visual design, layouts, illustrations, character work, and animation shown here were
        created by Katja Turnsek. The EKV hardware products were designed by Logina’s technical team
        and illustrated by Katja Turnsek.
      </p>
    </div>

